This is a fundamentally different vision of UBI.

It does not require pretending that money comes from nowhere.

It asks whether the United States can systematically convert a portion of the enormous wealth generated by its economy into an enduring capital base owned on behalf of its citizens.

The fiscal literature already identifies multiple possible UBI funding mechanisms, while Alaska provides an important real-world example of turning collectively controlled resource wealth into recurring citizen payments. 

The major unanswered question is therefore not whether a $1,000-per-month UBI can be imagined.

It can.

The harder mathematical question is:

What combination of taxes, public assets, sovereign investment, technological rents, natural-resource rents and productivity gains can sustainably generate the required cash flow without destabilizing the economy?

That is where Mathematical Futurology can become useful: not as a prediction that one particular UBI model will work, but as a framework for continuously modeling population, productivity, inflation, taxation, investment returns, government expenditure and national wealth until policymakers can see which combinations are fiscally sustainable.
